  <prompt>
    Implement FR-11 + FR-12: promote `w14:paraId` to the PRIMARY anchor for annotations/redlines and
    establish it as the splice key for the E1 delta save. Two coupled outcomes.
    (1) FR-11 — annotations/redlines resolve by `paraId` first; the existing `AnnotationReanchorService`
    (textPattern + Levenshtein + paragraphHint) is RETAINED as the **cross-Word-session fallback**.
    This retention is mandatory, not optional: Word regenerates ALL paraIds on save when tracked
    changes or comments are present (Open-XML-SDK #925), so paraId is stable only within OUR own
    load→edit→save round-trip and is gone after an external Word edit — the fuzzy matcher
    re-anchors in that case (design §5.2 caveat). Resolution order: try paraId, fall back to content
    match when the paraId is absent.
    (2) FR-12 — on save, paraId is the key that maps each edited editor paragraph to the original
    OOXML paragraph for the FR-02 splice (an edit to paragraph P updates exactly the original
    paragraph with the matching paraId).
    The change is ADDITIVE: add a `paraId` field to `AnchoredAnnotationAnchor`
    (`compose-contracts.ts:108-115`; `textPattern`/`paragraphHint`/`spanId` remain) and extend the
    server `AnnotationReanchorService.cs` to try paraId before the fuzzy score. This is the ADR
    Tension Path A item — `AnchoredAnnotation` gains `paraId`/offsets but stays Compose-domain
    (positional document-adjacent UI state, never written via `memory.*`).
  </prompt>

  <role>
    Full-stack engineer comfortable on both sides of the Compose seam: the TypeScript client
    contracts (`AnchoredAnnotationAnchor` in `compose-contracts.ts`) and the .NET
    `AnnotationReanchorService` re-anchor scorer. Fluent in mirror-first additive contract evolution
    and in reasoning about anchor-resolution ordering (paraId-primary, fuzzy fallback).
  </role>

  <goal>
    Annotations/redlines carry an explicit `paraId` (additive on `AnchoredAnnotationAnchor`) and
    resolve by it first, with `AnnotationReanchorService`'s textPattern+Levenshtein+paragraphHint
    matcher retained as the cross-Word-session fallback (tried only when the paraId is absent). On
    save, paraId is the key mapping edited editor paragraphs to original OOXML paragraphs for the
    FR-02 splice. Tests prove: an anchor resolves by paraId within our round-trip; the SAME anchor
    re-resolves by fuzzy match after an external Word edit that regenerated paraIds; and an edit to
    paragraph P maps to exactly the original paragraph with matching paraId. The client mirror
    (`compose-contracts.ts`) and server contract stay in sync; a through-the-wire slice test (NFR-06)
    proves the anchor round-trips to the correct paragraph.
  </goal>

  <context>
    <background>
      E2 anchoring half — it consumes task 010's server paraId map and task 011's client-carried node
      attributes and turns paraId into the primary anchor + splice key (design §5, §5.2, §6.2, §8, §10).
      Today the anchor is fuzzy: `AnchoredAnnotationAnchor` = `textPattern` (content match) +
      `paragraphHint` (best-effort index) + `spanId` (session-only), re-scored with Levenshtein by
      `AnnotationReanchorService.cs` (design §2). The design does NOT retire the fuzzy matcher — it
      PROMOTES paraId to primary and KEEPS the matcher as the fallback because Word regenerates all
      paraIds on external save (Open-XML-SDK #925), so the two compose cleanly: try paraId first,
      fall back to content match when the paraId is gone. paraId is ALSO the splice key the E1
      Docxodus patch uses (FR-02/FR-12) — the same substrate serves anchoring and the save splice. The
      contract change is additive and mirror-first: `paraId` on the client `AnchoredAnnotationAnchor` +
      the server anchor. This is ADR Tension Path A (inherited from R2): `AnchoredAnnotation` gains
      `paraId`/offsets but stays Compose-domain positional UI state — never written via `memory.*`
      (design §11; project CLAUDE.md ADR Tensions). This task touches `AnnotationReanchorService.cs`
      (a shared Compose seam) so it is parallel-safe=false; run `/conflict-check` before the BFF PR.
    </background>
